---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';

// Tools from our Malaysia directory by MyInvois-integration status, as each
// vendor states it (checked 30 Sep 2026 against site/src/data/international/my.json).
// BuildSpace describes no e-invoicing; IFCA says it submits by direct API.
const tools = [
  { name: 'AutoCount', status: 'Direct submission to MyInvois through its own e-Invoice Platform (standard, consolidated and self-billed), queuing and retrying while MyInvois is down, AutoCount says', fit: 'Desktop or cloud accounting', note: 'Cloud from RM70/month excl. SST; desktop via dealers, no public prices', href: '/my/autocount/' },
  { name: 'SQL Account', status: 'One-click submission to MyInvois, with consolidated and self-billed e-invoices, E Stream says; a QR code lets customers enter their own details', fit: 'Desktop or cloud accounting', note: 'Cloud from RM79/month; desktop via partners, no public prices', href: '/my/sql-account/' },
  { name: 'Bukku', status: 'One-click MyInvois submission on every plan, including the free one, Bukku says; its developer is a Peppol certified access point (MDEC)', fit: 'Cloud accounting; free Launch plan for new companies', note: 'Launch free; paid RM35-RM135/month plus 8% SST', href: '/my/bukku/' },
  { name: 'Financio (ABSS)', status: 'Send, receive and track e-invoices with LHDN, Financio says; Essentials includes 25 LHDN e-invoices, Premier 5,000', fit: 'Cloud accounting and payroll for SMEs', note: 'RM50 or RM85/month; SST treatment not stated', href: '/my/financio/' },
  { name: 'HashMicro', status: 'HashMicro says its e-invoicing is integrated with MyInvois; construction module with BoQ templates and progress billing', fit: 'ERP buyers', note: 'Quote-based', href: '/my/hashmicro/' },
  { name: 'Globe3 ERP', status: 'E-Invoice Malaysia module for LHDN submissions, Globe3 says; construction project costing and progress claims', fit: 'ERP buyers, including contractors', note: 'Quote-based', href: '/my/globe3-erp/' },
  { name: 'BuildSpace', status: 'No e-invoicing features described: covers tendering, estimating and progress evaluation, so pair it with accounting software for LHDN', fit: 'Developers, contractors and consultants', note: 'Quote-based', href: '/my/buildspace/' },
  { name: 'IFCA (ContractX)', status: 'IFCA says its ERP suite, including ContractX, submits e-invoices to LHDN by direct API; ContractX covers progress claims and retention', fit: 'Contractors running multiple projects', note: 'Quote-based', href: '/my/ifca-msc/' },
];

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Is MyInvois mandatory yet, and for whom?', a: 'It is mandatory, but phased by annual turnover. It went live for the largest firms (>RM100m) on 1 Aug 2024, then >RM25m on 1 Jan 2025, >RM5m on 1 Jul 2025, and firms up to RM5m on 1 Jan 2026. Since an LHDN update on 30 August 2026, businesses with annual turnover or revenue under RM3 million are exempt (e-Invoice Guideline v4.8, section 1.6), so in practice the January 2026 phase now covers turnover from RM3m up to RM5m. The exemption does not apply to a business that has a non-individual shareholder, a holding company, or a related company or joint venture with turnover of RM3m or more, and LHDN says its exemptions are reviewed from time to time. New businesses have their own start dates: those that began operating in 2023 to 2025 with turnover of at least RM3m start on 1 July 2026, and those starting from 2026 start on 1 July 2026 or when operations begin (or, if first-year turnover is expected to be under RM3m, from 1 January of the second year after turnover reaches RM3m). Exempt businesses can still join voluntarily. For firms up to RM5m, an interim relaxation runs to 31 December 2027, allowing consolidated e-invoices and no Section 120 prosecution for taxpayers who meet its conditions.' },
  { q: 'I\'m a contractor. Must I really e-invoice every progress claim separately?', a: 'Read this carefully. YES, construction is on the official "no-consolidation" list (Specific Guideline v4.9, 7 September 2026, Table 3.6 item 4: a "construction contractor undertaking a construction contract" per the Income Tax (Construction Contracts) Regulations 2007), which in principle means each progress/interim claim must be its own e-invoice rather than batched into a monthly consolidated one. BUT, the interim relaxation in §16.2 of the same guideline expressly permits issuing consolidated e-invoices "for all activities and transactions," construction included, during your grace window. For contractors up to RM5m turnover that window runs to 31 December 2027. So in practice: contractors up to RM5m can still batch claims into a consolidated e-invoice for now; larger contractors (>RM5m, already past their relaxation) must issue individually today; and once the relaxation ends after 31 December 2027, the no-consolidation rule applies to the up-to-RM5m band too. Separately, any single transaction over RM10,000 has been on the same Table 3.6 list since 1 Jan 2026 (item 7), so it follows the same relaxation, and construction-materials wholesalers and retailers are not on the current v4.9 list.' },
  { q: 'How does the system actually work (model and format)?', a: 'You submit through the free MyInvois Portal or via an API (directly, or through a Peppol-accredited service provider under the MDEC Peppol framework). Documents are structured XML or JSON built on UBL 2.1. IRBM validates in near real-time (generally under two seconds) and on success returns a Unique Identifier Number (UIN) plus a validation QR code for the visual PDF. You have a 72-hour window to cancel and reissue; after that, corrections need a debit/credit/refund-note e-invoice. Where consolidation is allowed, the consolidated e-invoice is issued within 7 calendar days after month-end. Buyers issue self-billed e-invoices for imports, foreign suppliers and payments to agents/dealers/distributors.' },
  { q: 'What happens if I don\'t comply?', a: 'Non-compliance with the e-invoice rules can lead to prosecution under Section 120 of the Income Tax Act 1967, as the LHDN e-Invoice Specific Guideline confirms. The relief: under §16.3 of the Specific Guideline, IRBM will not prosecute during the interim relaxation period, which for businesses up to RM5m runs to 31 December 2027, provided you issue the consolidated e-invoices that §16.2 allows. IRBM also runs an e-Invoice Special Voluntary Disclosure Programme from 7 July 2026 to 31 December 2027 (§17) for businesses that missed e-invoices or submitted ones with errors. Beyond prosecution, non-validated invoices undermine your records for audit and deductibility.' },
  { q: 'What about the new SST on construction?', a: 'Separate from MyInvois (and run by a different authority: RMCD/Customs, not LHDN), the SST expansion of 1 July 2025 brought construction work services into the service-tax net for the first time, at 6%. You must register once your taxable construction services exceed RM1.5 million in any rolling 12-month period. Key exemptions: residential buildings (and related common facilities) and government/local-authority projects are excluded, and non-reviewable contracts signed before 1 July 2025 got a 12-month transitional exemption. So a contractor can be in scope for MyInvois (income-tax side) and SST (consumption-tax side) at the same time, through two different portals.' },
  { q: 'Does my software need to be MyInvois-native?', a: 'It must reach LHDN by your phase deadline, but there are several routes. The Malaysian accounting tools in our directory (AutoCount, SQL Account, Bukku, Financio, Biztory) and the ERPs (HashMicro, Globe3, IFCA) say they send e-invoices to LHDN, and the developers of AutoCount, SQL Account and Bukku are Peppol certified access points under MDEC; the free MyInvois Portal is another route. BuildSpace, and the takeoff tools Cubicost and RIB CostX, describe no e-invoicing features, so they pair with an accounting tool for the actual submission. The right question for any vendor: "Do you submit to LHDN directly (or via a Peppol provider), and how do you handle consolidated vs individual e-invoices for a construction contract?"' },
];

const pageTitle = 'MyInvois E-Invoicing in Malaysia: 2026 Guide for Contractors';
const pageDescription = 'Malaysia\'s MyInvois (LHDN/IRBM) e-invoicing explained for the building trades: the phased turnover thresholds, the RM3m exemption threshold set in August 2026, and the construction point: construction is a "no-consolidation" industry, but the §16.2 relaxation lets ≤RM5m contractors keep batching progress claims until 31 December 2027. Plus the 6% SST on construction services and which tools submit to LHDN. Independent, updated 29 September 2026.';

const guideUrl = new URL('/my/guides/myinvois-e-invoicing/', Astro.site).toString();
const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Malaysia', url: '/my/' },
  { name: 'MyInvois e-invoicing for contractors', url: '/my/guides/myinvois-e-invoicing/' },
];

const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Article', headline: pageTitle, description: pageDescription,
  datePublished: '2026-06-20', dateModified: '2026-09-29', inLanguage: 'en',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'WrenchStack Research', url: 'https://wrenchstack.com/research/' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'WrenchStack', url: 'https://wrenchstack.com' },
  mainEntityOfPage: guideUrl,
};
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
};
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} alternateUrls={[{ hreflang: 'en', href: guideUrl }, { hreflang: 'x-default', href: guideUrl }]}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-orange-600">🇲🇾 Malaysia · Compliance guide</p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 sm:text-4xl">
        MyInvois E-Invoicing in Malaysia
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
        What Malaysia's national e-invoicing system means for the building trades: it's live and phasing in by turnover. The thresholds, the rule for construction progress claims, the consequences, and which tools say they submit to LHDN.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-500">By WrenchStack Research · Published June 2026 · Updated 29 September 2026 · Not tax advice; confirm your situation with LHDN/IRBM (hasil.gov.my) or your tax agent.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="mb-8 rounded-lg border-2 border-orange-300 bg-orange-50 p-6">
      <h2 class="mb-2 text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">⏰ The short version</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-800 leading-relaxed">
        Malaysia's <strong>MyInvois</strong> (run by LHDN/IRBM) is <strong>live and phasing in by turnover</strong>: the largest firms since August 2024, firms up to <strong>RM5m from January 2026</strong>, and, since LHDN's 30 August 2026 update, turnover under <strong>RM3 million exempt</strong> (with exceptions for firms tied to larger groups). You submit XML/JSON (UBL 2.1) via the free portal or an API; IRBM validates in under two seconds and returns a <strong>UIN and QR code</strong>. The contractor headline: construction <em>is</em> on the <strong>"no-consolidation" list</strong> (each progress claim its own e-invoice in principle), <strong>but</strong> the §16.2 interim relaxation lets firms up to <strong>RM5m keep issuing consolidated e-invoices until 31 December 2027</strong>. The consequence: non-compliance can lead to <strong>prosecution under Section 120</strong> of the Income Tax Act 1967, though there is no prosecution during the grace window for taxpayers who meet the conditions of the relaxation. The task: make sure your tool submits to LHDN, directly or via a Peppol provider.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 text-center">
        <div class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">RM3m+</div>
        <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600">in MyInvois scope (since the 30 Aug 2026 update)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 text-center">
        <div class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">31 Dec 2027</div>
        <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600">≤RM5m grace, consolidation still allowed</div>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 text-center">
        <div class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">&lt;RM3m</div>
        <div class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600">turnover exempt (group exceptions apply)</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10 prose prose-slate max-w-none">
      <h2>The timeline</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>1 Aug 2024</strong>: Phase 1, turnover &gt; RM100m (penalty relaxation ended 31 Jan 2025).</li>
        <li><strong>1 Jan 2025</strong>: Phase 2, &gt; RM25m up to RM100m (relaxation ended 30 Jun 2025).</li>
        <li><strong>1 Jul 2025</strong>: Phase 3, &gt; RM5m up to RM25m, <strong>not deferred</strong> (relaxation ended 31 Dec 2025).</li>
        <li><strong>1 Jan 2026</strong>: Phase 4, up to RM5m goes live; interim relaxation (consolidated e-invoices allowed, no Section 120 prosecution for taxpayers who meet its conditions) <strong>to 31 Dec 2027</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>1 Jul 2026</strong>: start date for new businesses (begun 2023 to 2025 with turnover of at least RM3m, or starting from 2026); the same relaxation to 31 Dec 2027 applies.</li>
        <li><strong>7 Jul 2026</strong>: IRBM's e-Invoice Special Voluntary Disclosure Programme opens (runs to 31 Dec 2027).</li>
        <li><strong>30 Aug 2026</strong>: LHDN updates the timeline: turnover under <strong>RM3 million is exempt</strong>, so Phase 4 now effectively covers RM3m up to RM5m.</li>
        <li><strong>31 Dec 2027</strong>: the interim relaxation for the up-to-RM5m band ends.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What the system requires</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Phased, not all-at-once.</strong> Your obligation date depends on your annual turnover band; new businesses have their own start dates, tied to the RM3m threshold.</li>
        <li><strong>Structured format.</strong> XML or JSON built on <strong>UBL 2.1</strong>; IRBM validates in near real-time (under ~2 seconds) and returns a <strong>UIN + validation QR code</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Several channels.</strong> The free MyInvois Portal, a direct API, or an API through a <strong>Peppol-accredited service provider</strong> (the MDEC Peppol framework).</li>
        <li><strong>Construction is a special case.</strong> It's on the no-consolidation list, but the §16.2 relaxation suspends that for everyone during the grace window (to 31 Dec 2027 for ≤RM5m).</li>
        <li><strong>Housekeeping rules.</strong> A 72-hour cancellation window; consolidated e-invoices (where allowed) within 7 days of month-end; self-billed e-invoices for imports/foreign suppliers.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why this matters for contractors specifically</h2>
      <p>
        The construction rule is worth stating precisely: construction is on the <strong>no-consolidation list</strong>, which sounds like "e-invoice every progress claim individually, now." In reality the <strong>§16.2 interim relaxation lets contractors up to RM5m keep batching claims into consolidated e-invoices until 31 December 2027</strong>, so the per-claim requirement bites today only for larger contractors (already past their relaxation), and for the up-to-RM5m band once the relaxation ends after 31 December 2027. Two more things land on contractors: any <strong>single transaction over RM10,000</strong> is on the same no-consolidation list (from 1 Jan 2026, with the same relaxation), and the construction software in our directory handles e-invoicing differently: <a href="/my/buildspace/">BuildSpace</a> covers tendering, estimating and progress evaluation but describes no e-invoicing features, so it pairs with accounting software for MyInvois, while <a href="/my/ifca-msc/">IFCA</a> says its ERP suite, including ContractX for progress claims and retention, submits e-invoices to LHDN by direct API.
      </p>

      <h2>The wider Malaysian tax context</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>SST on construction:</strong> a new <strong>6%</strong> service tax on construction work services since 1 July 2025; register once taxable services exceed <strong>RM1.5m</strong> in a rolling 12 months. Residential buildings and government projects are exempt. Administered by RMCD/Customs, separate from MyInvois.</li>
        <li><strong>Currency:</strong> the Ringgit (MYR); set your software to Malaysia, not a regional default.</li>
        <li><strong>Employer duties:</strong> register with EPF/KWSP before the end of the first week of the first month in which you pay wages that attract contributions, and register yourself and your workers with SOCSO/PERKESO through its ASSIST portal, paying contributions by the 15th of the following month; see the <a href="/my/">compliance bodies</a> in our directory.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Which tools submit to MyInvois?</h2>
      <p>
        From the software in our <a href="/my/">Malaysia directory</a>, here's how the tools relate to MyInvois. In short: the accounting tools and ERPs listed say they send e-invoices to LHDN, IFCA's construction suite included; BuildSpace describes no e-invoicing features and pairs with accounting software for the submission. Always confirm current status with the vendor.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
        <thead class="bg-slate-50 text-left text-slate-700">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3 font-semibold">Tool</th>
            <th class="p-3 font-semibold">MyInvois status</th>
            <th class="p-3 font-semibold">Aimed at</th>
            <th class="p-3 font-semibold">Pricing</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100 bg-white">
          {tools.map((t) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-slate-900"><a href={t.href} class="hover:text-orange-600">{t.name}</a></td>
              <td class="p-3 text-slate-700">{t.status}</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-slate-600">{t.fit}</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-slate-600">{t.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-slate-500">MyInvois status reflects what each vendor says on its own pages, as checked for our directory on 30 September 2026; confirm current MyInvois and Peppol status directly with each vendor before relying on it.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10 rounded-lg bg-slate-50 p-6">
      <h2 class="mb-2 text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">The one question to ask any vendor</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
        Don't assume a good accounting tool submits to MyInvois, and if you run construction contracts, ask about claims handling. Ask: <strong>"Do you submit to LHDN directly or via a Peppol provider, and how do you handle consolidated vs individual e-invoices for a construction contract?"</strong> A vague answer is your signal. And confirm your own position by turnover: under RM3m you are exempt unless a group exception applies, and from RM3m up to RM5m your relaxation window ends on 31 December 2027.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="mb-5 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4">
            <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-slate-900">{f.q}</summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10 rounded-lg bg-slate-50 p-5 text-xs text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
      <strong class="text-slate-700">Sources &amp; verification:</strong> Updated 29 September 2026 against LHDN's e-Invoice Implementation Timeline page (updated 30 August 2026), the IRBM e-Invoice Guideline v4.8 (30 August 2026) and the e-Invoice Specific Guideline v4.9 (7 September 2026); first verified June 2026 against Specific Guideline v4.7 and the General FAQs (5 May 2026), cross-checked with EY Malaysia, Grant Thornton, Sovos and VATupdate. Legal basis: s.134A and s.120 of the Income Tax Act 1967. The phased thresholds, the RM3m exemption and its group exceptions (Guideline v4.8 section 1.6), the new-business start dates, the construction no-consolidation listing (v4.9 Table 3.6 #4), the §16.2 interim relaxation and §16.3 prosecution relief to 31 December 2027 for up to RM5m (v4.9 Table 16.1), the single-transaction RM10,000 rule (Table 3.6 #7), the voluntary disclosure programme (v4.9 §17), UBL 2.1 + UIN + QR, the 72-hour cancellation window and the possibility of prosecution under Section 120 are from these documents; per-offence penalty amounts were not confirmed in the 30 September 2026 check and are not stated here. The 6% SST on construction services (RM1.5m threshold, residential/government exemptions, RMCD-administered) is per RMCD/L&amp;Co. Corporate-tax rates and the SST treatment of mixed-development residential portions are not stated here as they were not pinned to a primary source. General information, not tax advice. Spotted an error? <a href="/contact/" class="underline">Tell us</a> and we'll correct it.
    </section>

    <section class="rounded-lg bg-slate-900 p-8 text-white">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Compare Malaysian trades software & services</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">Independent reviews of MyInvois-ready Malaysian software, government tender access, EPF/SOCSO payroll and Contractor's All Risks insurance, with no pay-to-play.</p>
      <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a href="/my/" class="rounded-md bg-orange-500 px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-orange-600">🇲🇾 Malaysia directory →</a>
        <a href="/my/compare/" class="rounded-md bg-white px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900 hover:bg-slate-100">Compare vendors</a>
        <a href="/for-vendors/" class="rounded-md border border-white/30 bg-white/10 px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-white/20">Are you a vendor? Work with us</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
